"The surah begins with the expression 'by the star.' Here, the word 'necm' has different meanings: a star, stemless plants, that which descends piecemeal (such as the Quran), or specifically the Pleiades (Ülker) star cluster. Based on the expression 'the Lord of Sirius (Şi'ra)' mentioned in verse 49, some exegetes have stated that 'necm' here is Sirius. The word 'heveyân', on the other hand, means the rising or setting of a star, something descending rapidly, or shooting upwards. The oath may have been sworn upon this movement of the star or upon the descent of the Quran.\n\nThe second verse is: 'Your companion has neither strayed nor erred.' 'Dalal' is the opposite of guidance (hidayah), while 'gayy' is the opposite of right judgment (rushd) and signifies losing the intellect's rectitude. By 'sahib' (companion), the Prophet (peace be upon him) is meant. This address is directed to the Quraysh, who said of him, 'He fabricated the Quran himself.' It is emphasized that the Prophet has neither strayed nor erred—that is, he has neither lost his way nor lost his reason. He does not speak out of whim (hevâ, according to the desire of his lower self); what he says is nothing but revelation.\n\nIn the third through seventh verses, the nature of revelation is described. The one who taught the Quran revealed to the Prophet is an angel 'of mighty power' (şedidü'l-kuvva), who is Gabriel (Cebrail). Although some say that the teacher is Allah, the majority hold the view that it is Gabriel. Gabriel is characterized as 'zûmirre' (possessor of soundness: strong, robustly built, sound in intellect). He stood erect (istivâ) on the highest horizon, and then drew near and descended downwards (tedellî). This describes the manner in which revelation arrived. Some commentators consider istivâ and tedellî to be an indication of the Prophet's Ascension (Mirac).\n\nThe eighth and ninth verses depict closeness: 'He became distant two bow-lengths or nearer.' 'Kavs' means a bow, and 'kâb' is the distance between the grip and the bowstring. When making an alliance, the Arabs would join the grips of two bows together, which symbolized the tightest intimacy. This expression denotes the supreme degree of spiritual proximity between the Prophet and Allah. The tenth verse states that Allah revealed unto His servant. It is narrated that among the matters revealed directly to the Prophet without an intermediary during the Mirac were the five daily prayers and the concluding verses of Surah al-Baqarah.\n\nThe eleventh verse states: 'The heart did not deny what it saw.' What the Prophet saw was not an illusion, but a reality attested to by the heart. In the twelfth verse, the polytheists are rebuked for disputing over what was seen. The thirteenth verse notes that the Prophet saw Gabriel once more; this occurred while descending from the Mirac. 'Sidre-i Münteha' (Sidrat al-Muntaha), mentioned in the fourteenth verse, means the lote tree of the utmost boundary. 'Sidre' is a tree resembling the Arabian jujube, and 'münteha' denotes the final destination. This tree represents the ultimate boundary in Paradise, and it is narrated that the Prophet met Gabriel there.\n\nSidre-i Müntehâ is a lote tree (nebk) located in the seventh heaven. This tree is the source from which the rivers of Paradise promised to the righteous emerge. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) likened its fruit to the tassels of a crown and its leaves to the ears of elephants. According to narration, even if a rider were to travel in its shade for a hundred years, he could not traverse it, and a single leaf of it would cover the entire community. In a narration from Ibn Mas'ud, it is stated that the sidrah is at the outer edges of Paradise, and upon it are Sündüs and İstebrak (fine and heavy silk). In al-Kashshâf, it is likewise expressed that it is situated at the terminus of Paradise. According to Ibn Abbas and Ka'b, the sidrah is a tree beneath the Throne; the knowledge of angels, prophets, and scholars ultimately reaches it, and beyond it is the unseen (gayb). Dahhâk states that every command of Allah reaches the sidrah and does not pass beyond it.\n\nFakhr al-Din al-Râzî interpreted sidre-i müntehâ in the sense of 'the utmost bewilderment': at a station where intellects are astonished to an unimaginable degree, the Prophet was not overcome by bewilderment, was not confused, and saw what he saw. However, Râzî accepts the first narration as sound. Abu's-Su'ud, on the other hand, relying on the verse 'And that to your Lord is the final goal,' states that what is meant by müntehâ is Allah, and that sidrah, through annexation (izafet) to Allah, means 'the sidrah of Allah.' Jannat al-Ma'wa (Cennetu'l-Me'vâ) is beside this sidrah, and it is the Paradise to which the righteous and the martyrs will arrive.\n\nAs related in the hadiths of the Mirac, when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saw the sidrah, an abrupt transformation occurred when there covered it from Allah's command that which covered it, and it entered a state that no one could describe. In Ibn Jarir's narration, the Prophet saw that a golden moth covered it and that an angel on every leaf was glorifying Allah. In the narration from Ibn Abbas, it is related that Allah covered it and that Muhammad saw the greatest of the signs of his Lord. Rabi' states that the light of the Lord and the angels enveloped the sidrah; Abu'l-Aliyah states that it was enveloped in the light of Allah; and Mujahid states that Muhammad saw both the sidrah and, with his heart, saw Allah.\n\nThe verse 'The sight did not swerve' reports that the Prophet's gaze did not waver, did not swerve to the right or left, and did not exceed the limit of vision. Even while beholding astonishing things that would baffle intellects, he neither swerved nor transgressed the boundary. This demonstrates his adab and fortitude. Râzî contrasts this circumstance with Moses (Musa): the mountain that Moses saw was leveled to dust and Moses fainted, whereas the sidrah stood firm and Muhammad was not shaken. The verse 'He certainly saw of the greatest signs of his Lord' expresses certainty. 'Âyet' carries the meaning of marvels. Here, the meanings of 'the greatest sign' or 'the greatest among the signs' are present. During the Mirac, the Prophet saw the greatest sign among the signs of Lordship (rububiyyet) and the wonders of the dominion (mülk)—wonders that cannot be confined within the bounds of verbal speech and can only be attained through direct witnessing (müşahede). In this verse, it is not said that he saw his Lord, but rather that he saw the greatest of His signs; for this reason, the meaning of seeing Allah does not outwardly appear possible.\n\nIt is narrated in al-Bukhârî from Aisha: 'Whoever says that Muhammad saw his Lord has told a lie.' She then recited the verses al-An'âm 6/103 and Luqmân 31/34. However, in the narrations transmitted from Ibn Abbas, it is reported that the Prophet saw Allah. Al-Ash'arî and a group of scholars are of the opinion that the Prophet saw Allah with his physical eyes. Qâdî 'Iyâd states that seeing Allah in this world is rationally permissible and that there is no definitive proof against it in the sacred law (sharia). Râzî, however, indicates that the verse signifies that the Prophet saw Allah's signs rather than Allah Himself, yet points out that there is consensus that beatific vision (rü'yet) is permissible. Alî al-Qârî states that the proofs affirming rü'yet pertain to the manifestation of Allah's attributes, whereas the proofs negating it pertain to the manifestation of His essence (zât), and that manifestation of the essence is impossible. Ultimately, the sequence of the verses depicts the Prophet being taught through Gabriel, then ascending and surpassing Gabriel, and beholding the greatest signs.\n\nAfterwards, the idols worshiped by the polytheists are censured: Lât, Uzza, and Menat. These are nothing but names that you and your fathers have given; Allah has sent down no authority for them. They follow nothing but conjecture and the desires of their souls, even though guidance has already reached them from their Lord. Lât was located in Tâif, Uzza in Nahle, and Menat in Kudeyd. There were special shrines and caretakers for each of them. The Quraysh regarded Uzza as the greatest idol, visited it, and offered gifts and sacrifices to it. During the conquest of Mecca, the Prophet (peace be upon him) dispatched Khâlid b. al-Walîd, had the trees where Uzza was situated cut down, and had the idol destroyed. In this event, Khâlid encountered Dübeyye, the keeper of the idol, and killed him.\n\nElmalılı recounts the history of the idols Lat, Uzza, and Manat mentioned in these verses. Lat was the name of a rock in Ta'if; Uzza was an idol consisting of three trees near Mecca; and Manat was an idol erected at Qudayd between Medina and Mecca. All of these idols bore feminine names, and the polytheists regarded them as the daughters of Allah. During the time of the Prophet (peace be upon him), these idols were demolished one after another.\n\nDwelling on the feminine names the polytheists gave to their idols, Elmalılı states that the expression 'the male for you and the female for Him' conveys reproof. The polytheists had given feminine names to their idols because they believed that the angels were the daughters of Allah. This is described as 'a most unjust division.' In terms of Arabic grammar, it is explained that the word 'diza' means 'most unjust.'\n\nThe verses emphasize that the idols are 'nothing but mere names.' The idols that the polytheists praised as 'exalted cranes' (gharaniq) are in fact nothing at all. At this point, Elmalılı addresses the famous 'story of the Gharaniq' and proves that it is a fabrication. He states that it is impossible for the Prophet to utter anything outside of revelation and that this story has been rejected by the exegetes.\n\nAllah proclaims that there are numerous angels in the heavens, yet their intercession will be of no avail except by His permission. Those who do not believe in the Hereafter follow mere conjecture by giving feminine names to the angels. Conjecture, however, is of no avail against the truth. For this reason, the Prophet is asked to turn away from those who turn away from the Quran and desire nothing but the life of this world.\n\nThose who do good avoid major sins; minor faults are forgiven. Allah's forgiveness is vast. He created human beings from the earth and already knew them while they were in their mothers' wombs. Therefore, we should not claim purity for ourselves. Allah knows best those who guard against evil.\n\nThe verses point to individuals like al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, who turned away, gave a little, and then withheld stubbornly. The principles written in the scriptures of Moses (Musa) and Abraham are recalled: No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another, and man shall have nothing except what he strives for. The ultimate return is to Allah. It is He Who makes one laugh and weep, Who causes death and gives life, and Who enriches and impoverishes. He is the Lord of Sirius (Shi'ra). The earlier nations were destroyed.\n\nThe word 'kudyah' refers to hard rock that is difficult to dig. It is also used in meanings such as stinginess and harsh withholding.\n\nIn verse 35, it is questioned how the deniers could agree to take on sins even though they possess no knowledge of the unseen and have not witnessed the Hereafter.\n\nIn verses 36–37, the rulings found in the scriptures of Moses and Abraham are recalled. Abraham (peace be upon him) was a faithful prophet. According to Abu Hayyan, between Noah and Abraham, people used to assume one another's sins; Abraham stood against this.\n\nIn verse 38, the principle 'No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another' is emphasized. In Islamic jurisprudence (fiqh), this is grounded in the rule 'There is no representation/proxy in punishments' (al-niyabah la tajri fi al-'uqubat). Everyone bears the punishment for their own crime.\n\nIn verse 39, it is stated that a person will have nothing except the reward for what they have striven for. A person cannot earn spiritual reward through anything other than their own deeds. However, there are also gifts bestowed through Allah's grace. Things such as intercession and supplication depend on a person's faith. This verse has not been abrogated; according to the majority of scholars (jumhur), it is definitive (muhkam).\n\nIn verses 40–42, it is explained that a person will see the result of their striving in the Hereafter, that the fullest recompense will be granted, and that the ultimate destination is to Allah.\n\nIn verses 43–47, it is emphasized that Allah is the One Who makes one laugh and weep, Who causes death and gives life, Who creates male and female, and Who undertakes the second creation. This serves as evidence for the reality of the Hereafter.\n\nIn verse 48, it is stated that Allah is the One Who enriches and provides wealth.\n\nIn verse 49, it is stated that Allah is the Lord of Sirius (Shi'ra). In the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (Jahiliyyah), this star was worshipped; this belief is refuted by the verse.\n\nIn verses 50–54, the destruction of past nations is recalled: 'Ad, Thamud, the people of Noah, and the overturned cities (the people of Lot). These destructions were the consequence of the people's own sins.\n\nIn verses 55–56, human beings are asked which of their Lord's favors they doubt, and it is stated that these warnings are like the warnings of the earlier prophets.\n\nIn verses 57–58, the approaching Hour of Resurrection is described, and it is explained that none besides Allah can disclose or avert it.\n\nIn verses 59–62, it is recounted that the deniers marveled at the Quran, laughed instead of weeping, remained defiantly haughty, and were called to prostrate. The surah concludes with a verse of prostration (sajdah). According to narration, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prostrated here."